10:47:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can happen that a baby stays underwater for a minute or even longer and is completely fine—especially if the umbilical cord is intact, the placenta is still working well, and the baby is not stressed or trying to breathe yet. Your cousin’s experience is believable. The key point is that the baby was likely still being oxygenated through the placenta and cord during that time, not “breathing” the water or using the fluid in the lungs for oxygen. But a good outcome in several births does not make two minutes a reliably safe rule, because the important factors are not visible just from watching: - Is the placenta still delivering enough oxygen? - Is the cord compressed, stretched, or beginning to stop pulsing? - Was there fetal distress before delivery? - Is the baby actually relaxed, or becoming low on oxygen and about to gasp? - Has the placenta begun to detach? Those can vary dramatically from one birth to another. A baby may tolerate underwater time in one situation, while another baby with an unnoticed cord or placental problem could get into trouble quickly. Also, the “don’t breathe until exposed to air” reflex is real in healthy newborns, but it is not absolute. Low oxygen, infection, meconium, difficult labor, or stimulation can trigger gasping. If that happens underwater, the baby can inhale water. So: **yes, some babies have remained submerged for around two minutes without harm.** That shows it is physiologically possible under favorable conditions. It does not establish that it is generally safe to plan for, or that caregivers can assume every newborn will react the same way. That is why trained water-birth providers generally bring the baby to the surface promptly and monitor the baby and cord rather than deliberately waiting.
