10:48:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No—the fluid in the lungs is not what lets the baby get oxygen underwater. That timing can look persuasive because the baby may be underwater, lungs still fluid-filled, and appear fine. But the oxygen source is still the **placenta through the umbilical cord**. Before birth: - The lungs are fluid-filled and have very little blood flow compared with after birth. - They are not exchanging meaningful oxygen with the surrounding amniotic fluid—or with bath water after birth. - The placenta takes oxygen from the mother’s blood and sends it to the baby through the cord. The lung fluid itself is mostly fluid produced by the fetal lungs. It does contain some dissolved oxygen, like other body fluids, but nowhere near enough to sustain a baby. It is not an underwater breathing medium, and it is not continuously replenished with oxygen from the water outside the baby. A useful comparison: an adult’s lungs might be full of water during drowning, but that water does not supply usable oxygen. Having fluid in the lungs is not the same as getting oxygen from fluid. A newborn who stays submerged and remains well is generally doing so because: 1. the cord is still supplying oxygen from a functioning placenta, and 2. the baby has not begun air breathing or gasping underwater. Once the cord/placenta supply is no longer adequate, the baby cannot switch to extracting oxygen from water. It must inflate its lungs with **air**. If it tries to inhale while submerged, water can enter the airways. So the fluid-filled lungs and the underwater interval occur at the same time, but that does not mean one causes the other. The cord is the oxygen lifeline; the lung fluid is part of the normal fetal state that is about to be cleared as the baby begins breathing air.
