10:54:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: All water-birth babies do share the normal ability to tolerate fetal lung fluid during the transition. That helps explain why many are fine when they are brought up after birth. But it does not follow that the fluid itself is supplying oxygen or keeping every submerged baby safe. A shared ability to tolerate something is different from using it as a life-support system. For example, every fetus normally has fluid-filled lungs, yet before birth oxygen is still delivered through the placenta. If lung fluid were enough to oxygenate a baby, a fetus would not need placental oxygen in the first place—and a newborn with an interrupted cord could remain underwater safely. That is not how human physiology works. What varies among water births is things such as: - whether the cord and placenta are still delivering oxygen effectively, - whether the baby has begun the air-breathing transition, - whether there was stress or oxygen deprivation during labor, - whether the baby inhales or gasps while submerged. So the fluid deserves credit for being a normal, tolerated part of the transition—not for providing breathable oxygen. The fact that some babies stay underwater and are fine is real; it shows that they can sometimes remain stable briefly during that transition, not that the water or lung fluid is functioning as their oxygen source.
